course: euphemisms
lesson: 8
title: Soft words for hard things: the toilet and bodily functions and age and looks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: This lesson finishes the toilet words and starts the words for getting old.
explain: For the toilet, people pick a polite name ("lavatory"), a sailor's word ("the head") or a joke ("the smallest room").
explain: For age, people are kind ("golden years"), official ("senior citizen"), or cheeky ("no spring chicken", "over the hill").
explain: Vivid example: a birthday card for Dad's 50th shows a car rolling down a hill and says "Over the hill!" — he is not on a hill; the joke means he is getting old.
explain: Advertisers and officials like soft age words too: "mature students" or "senior discount" sound nicer than "old students" or "old people's discount".
fact: "The gents" is the everyday British name for the men's toilet.
fact: "The ladies" is the everyday British name for the women's toilet.
fact: "The head" is the sailors' word for the toilet on a boat or ship.
fact: "The smallest room" is a joking British name for the toilet.
fact: "Relieve oneself" is a polite way to say urinate or poo.
fact: "Take a leak" is slang for urinate.
fact: "Wee" is the British everyday word for urine or to urinate, often used with children.
fact: "Bowel movement" is the doctor's word for a poo.
fact: "Lavatory" is a polite, old word for the toilet.
fact: "The privy" is an old word for an outdoor toilet.
fact: "Senior citizen" is the official term for an old person, often over 60 or 65.
fact: "Senior" in America often means an old person, as in "senior discount".
fact: "Of a certain age" politely means middle-aged or older, without saying the number.
fact: "Getting on" means growing old.
fact: "Golden years" means old age, the years after retirement.
fact: "Twilight years" means the last years of a person's life.
fact: "Long in the tooth" is a joking way to say old; old horses' gums shrink, so their teeth look long.
fact: "No spring chicken" is a joking way to say not young any more.
fact: "Over the hill" is a joking way to say past one's best because of age.
fact: "Mature" in adverts and dating means older, for example "mature students".
exercise: In a British pub, where is "the gents"? | The men's toilet | The men's changing room | The bar for men only | The car park
exercise: In a British café, where is "the ladies"? | The women's toilet | A table for women | The kitchen staff room | A shop for women
exercise: A sailor says "I'm going to the head." Where is he going? | The toilet on the boat | The captain's cabin | The front of the boat | The top of the mast
exercise: Gran says "He's in the smallest room." Where is he? | On the toilet | In the cupboard | In the baby's room | In the attic
exercise: A sign says "Do not relieve yourself in the park." What must you not do? | Urinate or poo there | Rest on the grass | Take off your shoes | Feed the birds
exercise: A rough uncle says "Just gonna take a leak." What will he do? | Urinate | Fix a dripping tap | Steal | Check the car for oil
exercise: A British mum asks "Do you need a wee?" What is she asking? | If the child needs to urinate | If the child wants a small snack | If the child is tired | If the child wants a little toy
exercise: The doctor asks "Any change in your bowel movements?" What is the doctor asking about? | Your poo | How you move your arms | Your abs | How much you walk
exercise: An old sign on a train says "Lavatory". What is behind the door? | A toilet | A lab | A luggage room | A dining room
exercise: In an old farm story, what is "the privy"? | An outdoor toilet | A secret room | A chicken house | A garden shed for tools
exercise: A bus pass says "Senior citizen". Who is it for? | An old person | A town mayor | A school prefect | A city worker
exercise: A US cinema says "Senior tickets $6". Who can buy them? | Old people | Older children | Bosses | Teachers
exercise: An advert says "For women of a certain age." Who is it for? | Middle-aged or older women | Women born on a certain day | Girls under twelve | Women of any age
exercise: Mum says "Grandpa's getting on now." What does she mean? | He is growing old | He is boarding | He is getting along well with everyone | He is getting better after an illness
exercise: A leaflet says "Enjoy your golden years in the sun!" Who is it for? | Retired old people | People who like gold | Children on summer holiday | Lottery winners
exercise: A book says "She spent her twilight years by the sea." When did she live by the sea? | In the last years of her life | Only in the evenings | When she was a child | In the winter
exercise: "That horse is a bit long in the tooth." What does it mean? | The horse is old | The horse has big teeth | The horse bites | The horse needs a dentist
exercise: Dad laughs "I'm no spring chicken." What does he mean? | He is not young any more | He does not like chicken | He is not good at jumping | He does not like spring
exercise: A card for a 50th birthday says "Over the hill!" What does it mean? | Getting old and past one's best | Good at climbing | Going on a hiking trip | Living on a hill
exercise: A college welcomes "mature students". Who are they? | Older students | Very sensible children | Top students | Students who have finished
test: A sign at a Scottish station: "Gents ← and Ladies →". What is the sign for? | The men's and women's toilets | The men's and women's waiting rooms | Two shops | Two train platforms
test: "where's the head on this boat, I'm desperate" — what is the passenger looking for? | The toilet | The captain | The bow | The life jackets
test: A medical leaflet: "Fibre helps regular bowel movements." What does fibre help? | Pooing regularly | Moving your legs | Breathing better | Sleeping well
test: Brother: "stop the car, I gotta take a leak!" Mum: "Five more minutes." What does he need? | To urinate | To check a flat tyre | To fix a leaking bottle | To be sick
test: An old cottage advert: "Charming garden with original privy." What is in the garden? | An old outdoor toilet | A private pond | A secret path | A hidden treasure
test: Nan to the kids: "I'm getting on, I can't climb that." Why can't Nan climb? | Because she is old | Because she is getting on a bus | Because it is wet | Because she is busy
test: TV host: "Tonight, a pop star who's — let's be honest — no spring chicken." What is the host saying? | The pop star is not young | The pop star is a vegetarian | The pop star is not famous | The pop star cannot dance
test: Kid: "Dad's ancient, mate, proper over the hill." What does the kid mean? | Dad is old | Dad has gone hiking | Dad is far | Dad is very tall
test: A brochure: "Luxury cruises for the over-60s — your golden years deserve it!" Who is the cruise for? | Older, retired people | People who buy gold | Young couples | Families with babies
test: Jess reckons "long in the tooth" means someone has big teeth. Is she right? | No, it means old | Yes, it means big teeth | Yes, it means a sharp bite | No, hungry
test: An agency sign: "Mature actors wanted." Whom do they want? | Older actors | Very serious actors | Star actors | Well-behaved actors
test: In a novel: "The old lady excused herself to the smallest room." Where did she go? | The toilet | The pantry | A child's bedroom | The garden shed
test: A council leaflet: "Free swimming for senior citizens on Mondays." Who swims free? | Old people | Lifeguards | Teenagers | Council workers
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the nautical term "the head". | The toilet aboard a vessel | The forward section of a vessel | The principal officer aboard a vessel | The highest point of a vessel's mast
exam: It is stated that a gentleman is "long in the tooth". Ascertain the characteristic thereby attributed to him. | Advanced age | Large teeth | Dental ill health | An aggressive temperament
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the phrase "twilight years". | The final years of a person's life | The evening hours of each day | The years of early childhood | The years of greatest professional success
exam: Whereas a notice prohibits persons from "relieving themselves" in a public place, determine the conduct thereby prohibited. | Urinating or defecating | Resting upon public benches | Removing items of clothing | Consuming food and drink
